    thinking: "**Analyzing CSS Specificity**\n\nI\'m currently investigating why a Kanban card remained dark, even without the `.dark` class on the `<html>` tag. My focus is now on `src/index.css`. Specifically, I\'m examining how `@custom-variant dark` is implemented and how it impacts the CSS specificity within the context of the HTML structure and associated classes. I will have to look closely at the selectors!\n\n\n**Investigating Style Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now tracing the style issue back to the `index.html` file, specifically the `<body>` element and its associated classes. I\'ve discovered a discrepancy where elements were displaying dark styles even without the `.dark` class on the `<html>` tag. Further, I am focusing on the interaction of Tailwind CSS\'s dark mode implementation. A close look at `r1-tasks-kanban-light.png` shows the sidebar and a Kanban card with conflicting dark styles! Next up, I will check the `TaskListView.tsx` file.\n\n\n**Unraveling Style Inconsistencies**\n\nI\'m completely baffled! I\'ve been dissecting the inconsistent dark mode behavior in the Kanban card versus the `<aside>`. Both elements use the same class names (`bg-white dark:bg-slate-900`), yet one is white, one is dark. It\'s a mystery! I\'m now zeroing in on `TaskListView.tsx` and verifying that `@tailwindcss/vite` is processing it correctly. I need to understand why the default light mode style is being overridden on the card but not on the aside, even though both have identical classes.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Root Cause**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got it. I\'m now zeroing in on the interaction between Tailwind v4\'s dark mode and how it resolves class conflicts in the context of the Kanban board, as shown in the Playwright script. Specifically, I\'m verifying the path resolutions of Tailwind CSS from `/src/index.css`. The crux of the issue boils down to why the `<div key={task.id} className=\"p-3 rounded-lg bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 ...\">` Kanban card in `TaskListView.tsx` can be dark. I\'ll be checking the computed styles and revisiting the Playwright script.\n\n\n**Examining Timing Discrepancies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the asynchronous nature of the theme toggle function. I\'m focusing on why Playwright\'s `.click()` doesn\'t wait for the `fetch(\'/api/settings\')` request to complete. The inconsistency in screenshots is puzzling, especially considering the timing of the transition colors. I\'m considering scenarios where the screenshot captures the page during the transition. I will focus on `TaskDetailView.tsx` and `MihirWorkspaceApp.tsx` next. I will examine why the page could be partly white.\n\n\n"
