ion steps.\n- Expected result.\n- Actual result.\n- Status: Not Run, Passed, Failed, Blocked, Skipped.\n- Date and time.\n- Related screenshots or logs.\n- Reason for a blocked or skipped check.\n\nA blocked test must never appear as passed.\n\n7. GIT AND RELEASE WORKFLOW\n\nCreate a configurable, visual workflow for each repository attached to a task.\n\nSuggested stages:\n1. Confirm base branch.\n2. Update local base from remote.\n3. Create working branch.\n4. Implement changes.\n5. Review changed files.\n6. Run relevant checks.\n7. Commit locally.\n8. Push working branch to remote.\n9. Open pull request or merge request, where applicable.\n10. Merge into the development target.\n11. Verify the development result.\n12. Promote or merge to the configured cloud/release target.\n13. Verify cloud deployment or final environment.\n14. Complete the task.\n\nSupport direct merges and PR-based workflows through configuration.\n\nFor each stage, store:\n- Status: Not Started, In Progress, Completed, Blocked, Not Applicable.\n- Completion date and time.\n- Notes.\n- Relevant source and target branches.\n- Commit hash, PR/MR URL, or deployment URL.\n- Whether it was manually recorded or verified through an integration.\n\nMake the distinction between local commits, remote pushes, branch merges, and environment deployments clear.\n\n\342\200\234Cloud\342\200\235 may refer to a branch, remote repository, or deployment environment. Provide settings to name and configure the actual target rather than assuming it always means one thing.\n\nAllow stages to be marked Not Applicable with a reason. Allow corrections to previous stages and record those corrections in task activity.\n\nDo not show a task as fully delivered merely because a checkbox was clicked. Show remaining required checks and let me explicitly complete the task.\n\nFor the first version, manual tracking is acceptable and should work fully. Clearly label manually recorded progress. Do not pretend to execute Git commands or verify repository state.\n\nDo not automatically push, merge, or deploy from this application.\n\n8. TIME AND ACTIVITY\n\nAutomatically maintain:\n- Created timestamp.\n- Last updated timestamp.\n- Started timestamp.\n- Completed timestamp.\n- Git stage timestamps.\n- Work session history.\n\nProvide:\n- Start/stop task timer.\n- Manual time entries.\n- Editable work session notes.\n- Total time per task.\n- Daily and weekly work summaries.\n\nPersist an active timer across refreshes and prevent accidental duplicate timers.\n\nStore timestamps consistently and display them in my configured timezone, defaulting to Asia/Kolkata. Show dates in an unambiguous format such as 21 Oct 2026.\n\nKeep an activity timeline for status changes, notes, file updates, attachments, tests, and Git progress.\n\n9. PROJECTS AND SETTINGS\n\nAllow me to manage:\n- Projects and repositories.\n- Repository paths and remote URLs.\n- Default base and development branches per repository.\n- Optional cloud/release targets.\n- Workflow stages and required completion checks.\n- Tags.\n- Theme.\n- Timezone.\n- Attachment limits where relevant.\n\nTreat folder paths as useful records with copy buttons. Do not imply that a browser can open arbitrary local folders unless an actual supported integration exists.\n\n10. STORAGE AND BACKUP\n\nBuild a reliable single-user application suitable for running locally.\n\nUse a maintainable architecture with persistent storage for tasks, structured records, time entries, and attachment metadata.\n\nIf the workspace has no established stack, a reasonable default is:\n- React and TypeScript for the interface.\n- A small Node.js backend.\n- SQLite for structured data.\n- A local uploads directory for attachments.\n\nUse an existing suitable stack if one is already present.\n\nDo not store all important work only in browser localStorage. Use localStorage for small preferences if useful.\n\nProvide:\n- A complete backup export containing structured data and attachments.\n- Import/restore with validation and a clear duplicate-handling policy.\n- A readable task report export for sharing or keeping records.\n- Clear save failures and recovery behavior.\n- A documented location for the database and uploaded files.\n\n11. EXAMPLE CONTENT\n\nInclude a small optional demo dataset demonstrating:\n- Reordering Grafana security panels.\n- Improving graph loading behavior.\n- Adding an SSL/TLS \342\200\234Website certificate key\342\200\235 dropdown.\n- Updating related placeholder mapping in rayjobapp.\n\nUse these only as illustrative sample records. Do not claim they reflect my current repository status, completed merges, actual commits, or deployments.\n\nMake it easy to remove demo content.\n\n12. INTERACTION QUALITY\n\nEnsure:\n- New task creation is quick.\n- Detailed documentation can be added later.\n- Long forms are split into readable sections.\n- Important actions have clear feedback.\n- Destructive deletion has confirmation or an undo path.\n- Empty, loading, success, and error states are designed.\n- Forms have clear labels and validation.\n- Keyboard navigation and visible focus states work.\n- Text contrast is accessible.\n- Search and filtering remain usable as the task list grows.\n- Every visible button performs a meaningful action.\n\n13. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n\nThe application is ready when I can:\n- Create a task and find it again after restarting the application.\n- Attach multiple repositories with separate branch workflows.\n- Document the requirement, root cause, solution, and reasons for file changes.\n- Upload screenshots and reopen them after a restart.\n- Record passed, failed, and blocked tests accurately.\n- Track local commit, remote push, development merge, and cloud progress separately.\n- Log time and view task activity.\n- Search and filter my tasks.\n- Export a backup and restore its tasks and attachments.\n- Use the application comfortably in both light and dark themes.\n\nVerify the main workflow end to end using a realistic sample task. Include meaningful checks for persistence, attachments, workflow state, and backup/restore.\n\n14. DELIVERY\n\nDeliver:\n- The working application inside MIHIR.\n- Complete source code and dependency configuration.\n- Database initialization or migrations.\n- An example environment file if needed.\n- A README with exact installation and startup commands.\n- Backup and restore instructions.\n- A brief explanation of the implementation and any remaining limitations.\n\nMake sensible implementation decisions and complete the application. Prioritize a reliable daily workflow and a polished interface."
