  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/ultra_mode/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 99
    content: "---\nname: ultra-mode\ndescription: >-\n  Ultra mode is active for this conversation. Defines the mandatory build,\n  verify, critique and fix loop: plan acceptance checks, decouple and unit-test\n  pure logic (via node:test), make the app testable, look at the running app in\n  a real browser (via the computer-use skill), critique what you see against a\n  checklist, fix the top defects, and verify again before finishing. Read this\n  skill before your first code change in this conversation, and again only if\n  its instructions are no longer in your context.\n---\n\n# Ultra Mode: Verify What You Build\n\nUltra mode trades time for quality. The user accepts slower turns in exchange\nfor an app that has been **seen working**, not just compiled. Verification is a\nloop that runs during the build, not a final step.\n\nUse the `computer-use` skill for all browser mechanics (setup, opening pages,\nsnapshots, screenshots, videos). This skill tells you *when* to look, *what* to\njudge and *when* to stop.\n\n## 1. Before Writing Code\n\n1.  If you haven\'t already in this conversation, read the `computer-use` skill.\n    Because `playwright-cli` and headless Chromium are pre-installed in the\n    container, do not run its setup script before writing code; run the setup\n    script only if a `playwright-cli` command fails because it isn\'t installed\n    (e.g. after a container restart) or if you need to copy its extended\n    reference guides.\n2.  Write a short **verification plan** in your reasoning:\n    -   **Journeys**: the 1-3 most important things a user does in this app,\n        each as 2-5 steps with what must be *visibly true* after each step.\n        Example: \"type \'milk\' and press Enter -> \'milk\' appears at the top of\n        the list -> reload -> \'milk\' is still there\".\n    -   **Screens**: every distinct screen or state the request implies (start,\n        main, empty, loading, error, results, game over, ...).\n    -   **Reference**: if the user attached screenshots, mockups or a URL, list\n        what must match (layout, palette, lighting, typography, HUD).\n3.  Before writing code, read the matching reference file(s) in parallel:\n    -   For any **2D or 3D game**: read\n        [`references/game_title_and_hud.md`](references/game_title_and_hud.md)\n        (Title Screen, HUD, Game Shell & Gameplay Craft: controls, hitstop/juice\n        & encounter design).\n    -   For any **Three.js / WebGL or 3D scene** (including 3D games): read\n        [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md) (3D Surfaces, Scene\n        Layout, Lighting/Camera & Lossless Performance).\n4.  For follow-up requests, derive the journeys from what the user asked to\n    change, and re-check the existing journeys that the change could break.\n\n## 2. Make the App Testable\n\nBuild these in from the start. They cost little and make verification reliable:\n\n-   **Keep components modular**: Split views, forms, modals, and canvas\n    renderers into focused modules (e.g. under `src/components/` and\n    `src/utils/`, `components/` and `lib/`, or `src/app/`) rather than placing\n    the entire app in one large file. Smaller files make verification-round\n    fixes fast and surgical.\n-   **Build accessible UI. The browser sees your app through it.** Browser\n    snapshots are built from the accessibility tree. Each control shows up with\n    its role and accessible name (e.g. `button \"Add to cart\" [ref=e7]`), which\n    is how you find it and click it. Accessible UI also helps real users.\n    -   Use native elements: `<button>`, `<a href>`, `<input>` with a `<label>`,\n        `<select>`, headings, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<dialog>`. Never make a\n        clickable `<div>`. It shows up only as an unnamed `generic` node.\n    -   Every control needs a visible label or an `aria-label` (icon-only\n        buttons in particular). Make names unique and specific, e.g. \"Delete\n        \'milk\'\" rather than five buttons all called \"Delete\".\n    -   Use ARIA only where native HTML has no equivalent, e.g. tabs or\n        `aria-expanded` on a disclosure. Wrong ARIA is worse than none.\n-   Add `data-testid` only where the accessibility tree can\'t help: canvas or\n    WebGL surfaces, or containers you need to check by count or state. Snapshots\n    don\'t show `data-testid`. Use it in scripts that run against the page\n    (`getByTestId`).\n-   Ship deterministic demo or seed data, so journeys work without real\n    accounts, API keys or network responses\342\200\224but never bypass form validation by\n    silently substituting fallback values when required inputs are empty or\n    invalid.\n-   Wire actions and controls end-to-end: submitting a form, completing a flow,\n    or toggling a control must update the underlying application state (lists,\n    counts, statuses, active views) rather than only showing a static toast or\n    modal.\n-   Apps with intro or menu screens (games, onboarding, wizards) accept a\n    `?autostart=1` query parameter that skips straight to the main view.\n-   Canvas, WebGL and Three.js content is invisible to snapshots. Verify it with\n    screenshots or video, and expose the key state as attributes (e.g. `<body\n    data-state=\"playing\">` or a score element with a label).\n-   The browser here has no GPU, so WebGL / Three.js apps take the\n    software-WebGL path from the `threejs` skill (`isSoftwareGl`, on-demand\n    rendering, `afterDraw()` game loops); see\n    [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md) for budgets.\n-   For games and Three.js/WebGL scenes: follow\n    [`references/game_title_and_hud.md`](references/game_title_and_hud.md) and\n    [`references/games_3d.md`](references/games_3d.md): mount the\n    `WebGLRenderer` and scene once (`useEffect(..., [])`), storing React\n    callbacks/props in `useRef` so HUD state updates never tear down the canvas\n    or reset player position; never call React `setState` at 60Hz inside\n    `requestAnimationFrame`; listen for WASD/arrow keys on `window`; and spawn\n    the player on an open path (>= 2 tiles wide) facing a clear direction, never"
    num_lines: 276
    num_bytes: 14651
